to reducing the driver’s distraction caused by reading vehicle informa­ tion while driving (Ward and Parkes, 1994; Grandi et al., ,2022; Choi et al., 2002). Since the HUD is placed in the driver’s front field of view (FFOV), it is favourable to reduce the occupation of visual, motive and cognitive resources. According to references (Gibbered et al., 2010; Wang et al., 2016; Bellazzi et al., 2022), head-up display technology can decrease vehicle crashes by 25%. Augmented reality (AR) is a new type of human–computer interaction technology based on virtual reality (Fan and Liang, 2012; Fro¨hlich, 2010). Combining AR technology and HUD enables vehicles to carry both natural and artificial displays at the same time. It superimposes virtual information such as road conditions, nav­ igation, and non-geometric information of real objects (e.g. pedestrians crossing the road) to real driving scenes (Smith et al., 2017; Lee et al., 2009). Due to its characteristics of combining virtual and real, and realtime interaction, it has played a strong driving role in the development of future vehicle assisted driving in-vehicle entertainment and naviga­ tion (Choi et al., 2013; Hwang et al., 2015).
In recent years, the implementation of AR technology in IVIS has become a hot research topic for major automobile companies. Experts and scholars have conducted a lot of experiments on driver assistance systems based on AR and HUD technologies, and have achieved many results. Park (Park and Kim, 2014) developed a vehicle-mounted ARHUD system that can recognize driving safety information and project it into the driver’s FOV. The recognition rate of driving safety information can reach 73%, and the recognition speed can achieve 15 fps. Changrak Yoon et al. (Yoon et al., 2014) proposed a pre-collision warning system that combines AR technology and head-up display. The system detects vehicles and pedestrians ahead, judges dangerous driving situations based on the detection results, and uses AR technology demonstrations to warn drivers. Bark K (Bark et al., 2015) et al. proposed an AR-HUD navigation assistance system design interface. By comparing the driver’s reaction time in two situations with and without AR, the experiment result showed the AR-HUD navigation system can help the driver respond to the turning position faster. Lee JW (Lee et al., 2015) et al. summarized the problems of vehicle AR-HUD systems in display equipment, target recognition equipment, information organization and visual design methods, and experimental methods. Kim H et al. (Kim et al., 2016) used HUD to design a new pedestrian detection interface, and the virtual shadow of the pedestrian was covered by the front windshield in the driver’s FOV. The results showed that applying the system interface resulted in better driving performance and smoother braking behaviour. Hwang Y et al. (Hwang et al., 2016) tested the impact of the AR-HUD system on the driver’s risky-response ability and the driver’s psychological change. Kim K (Kim and Hwang, 2017) verified through experiments whether the AR-HUD system can effec­ tively improve driving safety and whether the visual symbols covered in the real driving environment will interfere with the driving vision. Zhang Huawei (Huawei, 2015) used various methods of ergonomics and design psychology to explore the design of the visual interface of the head-up display system to improve the driver’s cognitive load and driving safety.
A comprehensive analysis of the research status found that there are two main aspects to the application of AR and HUD technology to IVI­ S:①Apply AR and HUD technology to the forward-collision warning system, project the detected information of obstacles such as pedestrians and vehicles on the front windshield, and combine with the real driving environment to improve the driver’s situation awareness of risky driving ability; ②Apply AR and HUD technologies to the vehicle navigation system, and the visual navigation information is directly covered in the road environment to improve the intuitive and easy-to-read navigation system. Most of the research experiments focus on evaluating the effectiveness of the proposed system prototype by comparing the driving performance in different driving situations. However, there is a lack of further discussion on the impact of the AR-HUD system on improving driving distraction and the cognitive and decision-making process in dealing with risky driving situations. As a consequence, the main

